Z||000008||TRiAD||12-16-2001||09:41 PM||||Actually, the US is just simply THE most expensive country to certify a car for...<P>Our emissions standards alone have surpassed the rest of the world, and DOT generally requires <B>4</B> complete cars for the crash testing process...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fierokid87||12-16-2001||09:44 PM||||How many Cars did they use when they did the Fiero crash tests?<P>Scott||||reg||4
Z||000010||California Kid||12-16-2001||10:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierokid87:<BR><B>How many Cars did they use when they did the Fiero crash tests?<P>Scott</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Typically from the start of a new program until it's launched into production the automaker will crash about 75 "bucks"(not fully built vehicles) and about 100 fully assembled vehicles leading up to government certification tests. This all varies depending on how many engineering changes that may affect crash results have to go into the vehicle during it's development. This doesn't take into account all the testing of sub assemblies that undergo "bench tests" in prototype mode, it's a very costly process.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||12-17-2001||11:42 AM||||CK is right, for LARGE manufacturers. Most of those tests are in the development stage, and not for the gov't, however. <P>The government has 5 crash tests they perform; frontal offset, frontal center, side, rear, crush (roof) test. <P>There has only ever been ONE car that used the same chassis for more than one test, and they actually used the same chassis for ALL the tests. <P>Vector W8 Twin Turbo. Mr. Wiegert wanted to have them try to use the first chassis for the first two tests (they did not do frontal offset at that time), and they said they would "see how it does", they were trying to blow him off. It survived the 30mph frontal completely intact. The nose structure took ALL the damage, leaving everything else intact. So, they did the side impacty test. Same story. It passed, with flying colors, and the rest of the car was perfect. Well, the rear was the same way, so on to the roof crush. <P>The car scored 5 stars in all tests, and is the only car in history to ever do that, and the only one to pass all crash certifications with ONE car. <P>Now, the McLaren F1 could have done probably 2 or 3 tests with one chassis (they rolled one in desert testing at 220mph, and the driver walked away), but after the Vector, the gov't changed the rules. Dunno why. <P>||||reg||1
